1.Distributed Intrusion Detection & Prevention System (DIDS/IPS)

Introduction

A Distributed Intrusion Detection & Prevention System monitors network traffic from multiple systems, detects suspicious activities, and automatically blocks or responds to potential attacks.

Functional Requirements

• Monitor network traffic from multiple nodes.
• Capture and analyze packets.
• Detect known attacks using security rules.
• Detect abnormal network behavior.
• Generate security alerts.
• Calculate risk/severity levels.
• Block suspicious IP addresses or connections.
• Maintain security logs and reports.

Non-Functional Requirements

• Real-time detection.
• High availability.
• Low detection latency.
• Scalable.
• Reliable.
• Secure.

How it Works
Network traffic enters the monitoring nodes.
Packet Capture Engine collects network packets.
Traffic Analyzer examines packet information.
Detection Engine checks traffic against attack signatures and behavioral rules.
If suspicious activity is detected, a risk score is calculated.
Alert Manager generates a security alert.
If the threat is critical, Prevention Module blocks the suspicious IP or connection.
All events are stored in the centralized security log.
Key Design Decisions

• Packet Capture Engine collects network traffic.
• Detection Engine identifies malicious activities.
• Redis stores temporary counters and frequently accessed information.
• Kafka can transfer security events between distributed components.
• Elasticsearch can be used for fast security-log searching.
• Dashboard displays alerts, attacks, and network activity.

Challenge

Processing large amounts of network traffic in real time while maintaining low latency and minimizing false positives.

2. AI-Based Phishing Detection & Automated Response System
Introduction

An AI-Based Phishing Detection System identifies malicious emails, websites, and URLs using machine learning and security analysis. It automatically assigns a risk score and can quarantine dangerous content.

Functional Requirements

• Analyze emails and URLs.
• Detect suspicious links.
• Analyze domain and website characteristics.
• Identify phishing patterns.
• Use machine learning for classification.
• Calculate phishing risk score.
• Generate security alerts.
• Quarantine suspicious emails or URLs.
• Maintain detection history and logs.

Non-Functional Requirements

• High detection accuracy.
• Low detection latency.
• High availability.
• Scalable.
• Reliable.
• Secure.

How it Works
Email or URL enters the system.
URL Analyzer extracts the domain, URL structure, and other features.
Domain Reputation Service checks the reputation of the domain.
ML Detection Engine analyzes the extracted features.
System calculates a phishing risk score.
If the score is low, the request/email is considered safe.
If the score is high, an alert is generated.
Highly suspicious emails can be moved to quarantine.
Detection details are stored in the security database.
Key Design Decisions

• Machine Learning Model detects phishing patterns.
• URL Analyzer extracts suspicious URL features.
• Redis caches frequently checked domains.
• PostgreSQL/MySQL stores detection results and user information.
• Quarantine Service isolates suspicious emails.
• Dashboard displays phishing alerts and detection statistics.

Challenge

Detecting newly created phishing websites and sophisticated attacks that do not match previously known patterns.

3. Secure Cloud File Sharing & Threat Detection System
Introduction

A Secure Cloud File Sharing System allows users to upload, store, share, and download files securely while detecting malicious files and unauthorized activities.

Functional Requirements

• User registration and authentication.
• Secure file upload and download.
• File encryption.
• File sharing with authorized users.
• Access control.
• Malware/threat detection.
• File version management.
• Activity monitoring.
• Security alerts.
• Maintain audit logs.

Non-Functional Requirements

• High security.
• High availability.
• Scalable.
• Reliable.
• Fast file access.
• Data privacy.
• Fault tolerance.

How it Works
User logs into the system.
Authentication Service verifies the user.
User uploads a file.
File is scanned by the Threat Detection Service.
If the file is safe, it is encrypted and stored in cloud/object storage.
File metadata is stored in the database.
User can share the file with authorized users.
Access Control checks permissions whenever another user requests the file.
File activities are recorded in the audit log.
If suspicious activity is detected, the system generates an alert and can temporarily restrict access.
Key Design Decisions

• Object Storage stores large files efficiently.
• Encryption protects files during storage and transfer.
• PostgreSQL/MySQL stores users, file metadata, and permissions.
• Redis caches frequently accessed file metadata.
• Threat Detection Service scans uploaded files.
• Access Control Service manages user permissions.
• Audit Log Service records file activities.

Challenge

Providing secure and fast file sharing while protecting files from malware, unauthorized access, and data leakage.

4. Security Information and Event Management System (SIEM)
Introduction

A SIEM system collects security logs from servers, applications, network devices, and endpoints. It analyzes these events to detect threats and provides a centralized security monitoring platform.

Functional Requirements

• Collect logs from multiple devices and applications.
• Normalize and store security events.
• Detect suspicious activities.
• Correlate events from different sources.
• Generate security alerts.
• Calculate threat severity.
• Provide real-time monitoring.
• Maintain audit logs and reports.

Non-Functional Requirements

• Real-time processing.
• High availability.
• Scalable.
• Reliable.
• Secure.
• Low detection latency.

How it Works
Servers, applications, firewalls, and endpoints generate security logs.
Log Collection Service collects the events.
Log Processing Service normalizes the different log formats.
Events are sent to the Event Processing Engine.
Correlation Engine identifies relationships between multiple events.
Detection Engine identifies suspicious behavior.
Risk Engine calculates the severity of the incident.
Alert Service notifies security analysts.
Events are stored for investigation and reporting.
Key Design Decisions

• Kafka handles large volumes of security events.
• Elasticsearch provides fast log searching and analysis.
• Redis stores temporary counters and frequently accessed data.
• Detection Engine identifies suspicious patterns.
• Dashboard provides real-time security visibility.
• Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) restricts access to security data.

Challenge

Processing millions of security events while identifying important threats without generating too many false alerts.

5. API Security Gateway & Threat Detection System
Introduction

An API Security Gateway protects APIs from unauthorized access, abuse, malicious requests, and excessive traffic. It acts as a security layer between clients and backend services.

Functional Requirements

• Authenticate API users.
• Authorize API requests.
• Validate incoming requests.
• Apply rate limits.
• Detect malicious API traffic.
• Block suspicious requests.
• Generate security alerts.
• Maintain API activity logs.
• Monitor API performance.

Non-Functional Requirements

• Low latency.
• High availability.
• Scalable.
• Reliable.
• Secure.
• Fault tolerant.

How it Works
Client sends an API request.
Request reaches the API Gateway.
Authentication Service verifies the access token/API key.
Authorization Service checks user permissions.
Rate Limiter checks the request frequency.
Request Validation checks parameters and payloads.
Threat Detection Engine analyzes suspicious patterns.
Safe requests are forwarded to the backend service.
Malicious requests are blocked.
All API activities are stored in security logs.
Key Design Decisions

• API Gateway acts as the central security entry point.
• JWT/OAuth 2.0 can be used for authentication and authorization.
• Redis stores rate-limit counters and temporary session information.
• WAF can filter common web attacks.
• Kafka can process API security events asynchronously.
• Elasticsearch stores and searches API security logs.

Challenge

Protecting thousands of APIs from attacks while maintaining very low response latency.
